# Switch the DSA prefill empty-index-block guard from an any-valid reduce to a single
# first-slot test, and add an opt-in assertion for the invariant that makes it correct.
#
# SUPERSEDES rejected/04-dsa-prefill-first-slot-guard.patch, which is the same kernel change
# measured and rejected in round 1 on a correctness argument. Round 2 settled that argument
# empirically (see below) and adopts it.
#
# base:  applies ON TOP OF 01-dsa-prefill-skip-empty-index-blocks.patch (and is developed on
#        top of 01+02+03). Stacked, not independent: it edits the guard that 01 introduces and
#        will not apply to stock sglang.
#        Pristine chain, no git metadata needed:
#          patches/base/tilelang_kernel.py  (clean at sglang 2948168546)
#            -> 01 -> patches/applied/tilelang_kernel.py -> this patch
#          patches/base/dsa_backend.py      (clean at sglang 2948168546)
#            -> 01+02 -> patches/applied/dsa_backend.py -> this patch
#        The exact patched files are also kept verbatim at patches/arms/fs/, so a diff can be
#        produced without applying anything.
#
# apply: cd /sgl-workspace/sglang
#        git apply -p1 01-dsa-prefill-skip-empty-index-blocks.patch
#        git apply -p1 02-dsa-prefill-fused-q-fp8-prep.patch
#        git apply -p1 03-mla-absorb-drop-unit-weight-scale.patch
#        git apply -p1 dsa-prefill-first-slot-empty-block-guard.patch
#        (or: cp patches/arms/fs/{tilelang_kernel.py,dsa_backend.py} over the two files;
#         analysis/set_arm.sh fs does exactly that)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MEASUREMENT -- read this before quoting a number for it.
#
#   arm    sessions x runs   pooled tok/s   session means
#   r1ref  3 x 3             1508.187       1507.36 / 1507.76 / 1509.44
#   fs     5 x 3             1512.166       1511.11 / 1511.51 / 1510.46 / 1513.91 / 1513.84
#
#   delta +0.264%   (1508.187 -> 1512.166)
#
# Interleaved r1ref/fs/r1ref/fs/r1ref/fs, one fresh server per session, three benches per
# server, unmodified scripts/run_bench.sh at 192 prompts / concurrency 64 / ISL 8192 / OSL
# 1024. The two extra fs sessions (s8, s10) are the fs legs of the lead-2 A/B, same arm and
# same boot, pooled in here.
#
# THIS IS NOT CLAIMED AS A THROUGHPUT WIN. The delta is +0.264%, and the run-level
# peak-to-peak noise on this boot is 0.402% (r1ref) / 0.718% (fs) -- so the gain is smaller
# than the noise floor by the same peak-to-peak measure round 1 used to set its 0.39% floor.
# Individual runs from the two arms overlap.
#
# Note against my own case: adding s8/s10 moved the point estimate UP (+0.188% -> +0.264%)
# and the fs run-level p2p up as well (0.410% -> 0.718%). More data made the estimate more
# favourable and the bar it has to clear worse, and it still does not clear it. It is also
# partly boot drift: fs drifted ~0.2% upward over four hours (FINDINGS.md section 3), and s8
# and s10 are the two late sessions.
#
# What CAN be said, and is the reason it is adopted rather than shelved:
#   * the sign is consistent across all five independent restarts of fs;
#   * every fs session mean (min 1510.46) exceeds every r1ref session mean (max 1509.44);
#   * on session means -- the independent unit, since runs inside one server are correlated --
#     Welch gives t = 4.15, df = 5.7, diff = +3.98 tok/s, SE = 0.96 over all five fs sessions
#     (t = 4.03, df = 2.9 on the original three-vs-three);
#   * the change is strictly less work: the emitted kernel loses a cross-lane AllReduce and its
#     LDS workspace and gains a single scalar load. There is no mechanism by which it is slower.
# Round 1 extrapolated +0.64% from isolated kernel timings and explicitly did not measure it
# end to end. That extrapolation was optimistic; +0.264% is what it is worth here.
#
# accuracy: gsm8k 5-shot strict-match = 0.9704321455648218 +- 0.0046658931342207464
#           (scripts/run_eval.sh unmodified, 1319 problems, eval_results/fs_gate_20260820_202453).
#           That is identical to round 1's reference 0.970432 to every digit recorded.
#           Reference 0.970432 +- 0.004666, and the gate has ~+-0.005 of its own run-to-run
#           noise (round 1 measured this using a bit-identical patch), so this passes the gate;
#           it is not evidence that accuracy improved or degraded.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY IT IS CORRECT, AND THE CONFIGURATION THAT BOUNDS THAT CLAIM
#
# The first-slot test decides a 64-wide index block from slot 0 alone. That is equivalent to
# the reduce only if the -1 padding is a contiguous SUFFIX of each index row. Round 1 could not
# close that statically across the four topk backends and rejected the change. Round 2 measured
# it against live tensors instead, at ISL 8192, over one full benchmark:
#
#   rows checked            126,861,936
#   64-wide blocks checked  4,059,581,952
#   blocks skipped          491,606,928  (12.11%, reproducing round 1's independent 12.1%)
#   first_skips == reduce_skips                     exactly equal
#   blocks where slot 0 is -1 but the block has a real key        0
#   positions anywhere where a -1 is followed by a valid slot     0
#   rows that are PARTIALLY padded  31,700,466 (24.99%)  <- the discriminating case
#
# The last line is the one that makes this evidence rather than an assert that did not fire:
# a quarter of all rows straddle the padding boundary.
#
# Reachability. dsa_topk_backend is a SERVER ARGUMENT and the frozen launcher does not pass it,
# so it defaults to 'sgl-kernel'. The probe confirmed by direct observation that exactly one
# producer runs at prefill: sgl_kernel.fast_topk_transform_fused. The torch and flashinfer
# backends are not reachable without changing the launch configuration.
#
# AND THE INVARIANT IS NOT UNIVERSAL. _topk_unfused in dsa_topk_backend.py masks -inf scores to
# -1 in place, after the top-k, without re-sorting:
#     topk_local_indices = topk_local_indices.masked_fill(topk_scores == float("-inf"), -1)
# The torch backend survives only because torch.topk defaults to sorted=True descending, which
# pushes -inf to the tail. The flashinfer backend passes sorted=False, so the -1s land in
# arbitrary positions -- e.g. [-1, 0, -1, -1, -1, 1, 2, -1], where slot 0 is -1 while slots 5
# and 6 hold real pages. Under that backend this guard silently drops live KV keys.
#
# Hence the second half of this patch: SGLANG_DSA_ASSERT_TOPK_SUFFIX=1 (default OFF) checks the
# invariant and raises, naming the offending row. Cost when off is one predicted branch on a
# module-level constant per prefill layer call (~8k times per benchmark, ~0.25 ms). The
# assertion was verified to fire on scattered -1 and to stay silent on suffix-padded and
# all-padding rows.
#
# Decode is unaffected: skip_empty is only enabled at q.shape[0] >= 1024, and the largest
# decode query-token count observed over 12,168 decode calls at this workload is 512, so the
# guard is never compiled into the decode kernel. Round 1's prefill-only dispatch from patch 01
# is load-bearing and must stay -- the first-slot guard still costs ~4% at decode.
#
# ENGAGEMENT (the changed kernel is the one that runs). TileLang JIT-caches compiled kernels on
# disk, so this was verified rather than assumed: one new cache entry appeared the moment the
# patched server first ran a prefill chunk, and its generated HIP source contains
#     bool guard = (0 <= indices[(blockIdx.x * 2048) + (k_i * 64)]);
# with no any_valid symbol and only the two softmax AllReduces, against three AllReduces and an
# `int any_valid[1]` in every cached reduce-guard build of the same kernel signature.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
